WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
Good to have you here for First Principles Daily, episode 27, for July 2nd, 2026.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.380
Today we reason from raw materials, not analogy.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.660
The real price of artificial light has fallen roughly 10,000 folds since whale oil laps

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.400
because each generation measured lumens delivered rather than lamps sold.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:27.980
In 1800, a typical household paid the equivalent of several days' wages for the amount of light

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:33.520
now supplied by a single LED bulb costing a few dollars and drawing a few watts.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.760
The difference is not mainly cheaper lamps or cheaper electricity.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.800
It is the cumulative result of redesigning the conversion step itself, from burning fat

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:49.080
or oil to passing current through a semi-conductor junction that emits photons directly.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.660
Each stage attacked the largest loss term of the previous one.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:01:00.920
Heat, incomplete combustion or stokes shifted fluorescence were reduced rather than simply scaling up the old device.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:10.120
The pattern becomes clearest when the unit of account is shifted from the vessel or the fuel to the photons that actually reach the work surface.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:18.200
For centuries, the dominant method of producing light was to burn some form of fat or oil in an open flame or wick.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.040
The convention was inherited from pre-industrial practice.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:34.000
Find a combustible material that was already used for heat or cooking, put it in a simple vessel and accept that most of the energy would leave as heat and infrared rather than visible light.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:41.860
Candle makers and later whale oil refiners optimized for reliability and transport, not for photon yield.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.360
The physical limit of a flame was treated as fixed.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:55.040
Under this regime, the cost of usable light remained high, because the service, visible photons at the task surface, was never the unit of account.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:06.900
A typical whale oil lamp of the 1840s delivered perhaps one to two lumens per watt of chemical energy released, and the fuel itself was expensive to harvest and refine.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:16.400
Kerosene introduced after 1859, lowered the fuel cost dramatically, but still lost most of its energy to heat and soot.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:27.460
The lamp design itself changed only incrementally, when electric incandescent bulbs appeared, manufacturers again started from the existing socket and glass envelope.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:35.000
They optimized filament life and manufacturing throughput rather than asking how many watts were required to produce a lumen.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:45.220
The result was a product whose price tracked material and labor costs inside an accepted architecture instead of tracking the physical cost of the photons delivered.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:57.960
One objection often raised is that earlier societies simply lacked the materials or knowledge to do better, yet the same societies rapidly adopted kerosene once its supply chain existed.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:05.140
This shows that the barrier was not ignorance of chemistry, but the absence of a metric that made the waste visible.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.460
Another common view holds that incremental lamp improvements were sufficient.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:20.420
In practice, those tweaks stayed inside the combustion envelope, and therefore could not close the gap between chemical energy released and visible photons produced.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:33.960
Nordhouse's reconstruction shows that once the service is counted in lumens rather than in lamps or gallons, the price trajectory becomes visible as a series of discrete attacks on the dominant loss mechanism.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:38.980
The first clear break was the shift from animal oils to kerosene.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:47.540
The raw material cost per lumen hour fell because petroleum yielded far more combustible liquid per unit effort than hunting whales.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.480
Yet the combustion efficiency remained low.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:59.940
The next break was the incandescent lamp, which replaced chemical energy with electrical energy and allowed the radiating body to be raised to a higher temperature.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:06.020
Even so, roughly 95% of the electrical input still emerged as infrared.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:15.380
Florescent tubes attacked that loss by using a mercury discharge to generate ultraviolet photons that were then downconverted by phosphors.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:23.360
This raised system efficacy from roughly 15 limes seconds per w to 60 to 80 limes seconds per w.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:29.560
Each of these steps required a new physical mechanism, not merely a cheaper version of the old one.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.840
The LED continues the same logic.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:41.300
An electron hole recombination in a direct band gap semiconductor produces a photon whose energy is set by the band gap.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.260
So the stokes loss of fluorescence is largely eliminated.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.400
Early high brightness LEDs reach 30 to 50 limes per w.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:05:01.140
Modern phosphor converted white LEDs exceed 150 limits per w in production devices and approach 200 limits per w in laboratory records.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:10.120
The magic wand floor for white light is set by the energy of the photons themselves plus the minimum voltage required to drive the junction.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:21.900
A rough estimate places the theoretical maximum around 250 to 300 limes per w for a broadband white spectrum once all non-radiative paths are removed.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:32.000
On that basis, the idiot index of an 1800 terminator whale oil system was on the order of several hundred relative to the photon energy floor.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:41.400
Consider the arithmetic if the floor is roughly 300 lms per watt and a whale oil lamp delivered 1 to 2 lms per watt.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.360
The finished service costs 200 times or more the raw photon cost.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.020
That is an idiot index in the hundreds.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:54.860
A contemporary LED system sits perhaps a factor of 2 to 3 above the same floor.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:07.700
At 150 to 200 limes per w against the 300 limes per w ceiling, the ratio falls to roughly 2 already 2 orders of magnitude closer to the physical limit.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:12.040
The successive redesigns also change the supporting infrastructure.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:26.640
Kerosene needed distillation and distribution, incandescence needed generators and wiring, fluorescence needed ballasts, LEDs need only low voltage drivers and increasingly integrated heat sinks.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:33.800
Each infrastructure change was accepted only after the core conversion efficiency justified the capital outlay.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:38.780
One might ask whether the gains came simply from cheaper electricity.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:49.460
The data show that the largest drops in cost per lumen preceded the cheapest grid power and were driven instead by the fraction of energy converted to visible light.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.360
Another objection notes that early LEDs were themselves expensive.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:01.660
The price fell only after the semiconductor process moved from research devices to high volume wafer fabrication.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:10.760
This illustrates that the first principle step must still clear a manufacturing threshold before the idiot index can be driven down.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:23.940
By the early 21st century, the cost per megalumen hour had fallen by something like 4 orders of magnitude from 1800 levels when measured in constant dollars and constant purchasing power.

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:38.100
LED bulbs now retail for a few dollars while delivering 10 to 15 times the lumens per watt of a 1990s incandescent and roughly 100 times the lumens per watt of a kerosene lantern.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:42.060
The remaining gap to the photon energy limit is real.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:50.140
Thermal droop, phosphor conversion losses and driver inefficiency still dissipate roughly half the input power as heat.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:58.260
Further gains require either narrower band direct emitters for specific tasks or better thermal and optical extraction.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:04.460
Both of these are already visible in laboratory devices, but not yet at commodity cost.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:12.800
The tradeoff accepted along the way was greater dependence on complex supply chains for rare earth phosphors and gallium compounds.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:24.440
Those materials are not the dominant cost today. They illustrate that moving closer to the physical floor can shift the problem from energy waste to material specificity.

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:36.360
Even with those dependencies, the idiot index has dropped far enough that an LED system now sits only a small multiple above the energy required to create the photons themselves.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:42.520
Whereas the whale oil system sat hundreds of multiples above the same physical floor.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:54.340
Counting the photons that actually reach the task rather than counting the vessels that contain a flame or filament is what made each successive loss term visible and therefore attackable.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:09:08.160
Treating the entire chain from fuel or electricity to delivered lumens as a single conversion problem forced designers to abandon the previous mechanical envelope whenever a new mechanism offered a lower waste fraction.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:16.560
The same move remains open wherever an industry still prices the container instead of the service the container ultimately supplies.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:24.880
Which present day sector still sells the barrel or the fixture instead of the work the photons or electrons ultimately perform.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:34.900
That's first principles daily for today if this changed how you see what things cost share it with someone who depreciate it and subscribe wherever you listen.

